//
// Halo CE shader_model, used by objects: scenery, vehicles, weapons, characters.
//
// The base map is tinted by one of the object's change colors and combined with a detail map;
// a cube map reflection is added on top, and the diffuse color glows by an animated
// self-illumination color. The multipurpose map's four channels mask the reflection,
// self-illumination, change color and detail map, in an order that depends on the tag (Xbox or
// PC), so the material passes a channel selector for each mask.
//
// Objects weren't lightmapped in Halo; they were lit by the lightmap around them. A placed
// object's renderer tint holds its row in a table of per-object values (ObjectLightingTable):
// Halo's ambient, main and bounce lights worked out from the lightmap under it (see
// ObjectLightingSampler), a reflection tint, and the change
// colors A-D Halo picked for it. Objects without a row (models a game spawns) get Halo's
// lighting of a moving object from their renderer's attributes (HaloObjectAmbient and the rest,
// below), which the mount works out and eases for every Halo model in a scene
// (MovingObjectLights); until it has, from the map's light volume at their position
// (ObjectLightVolume), else with the map's average light. A tint other than white is their color,
// all four change colors (not until they have that lighting); a white one leaves change color A
// white and B-D from the material. The scene's point and spot lights (Halo's object lights) add to that as
// Halo's model vertex shaders added them, diffuse only (see HaloPointLights); the scene's own
// lighting only lights Halo models before any map is built. The reflection and self-illumination
// don't take light: like Halo, they're added to the lit color in gamma space (see ShadeHalo).
//
// Per-object attributes a game sets on the renderer (renderer.Attributes, which the scene object
// gets), all optional:
//   HaloChangeColorA-D (Vector3, 0-1): the object's change colors (armor team colors), over
//     whatever the table, tint or material says.
//   HaloLightProbe (Vector4): xyz = where in the world (s&box units) the object's lighting ray
//     starts (its bounding sphere center), w = 1 to use it. Without it, the mount uses the center
//     Halo would (the model's root bone and its object's bounding offset); the light volume
//     fallback reads a quarter world unit above the object's origin.
//   HaloObjectAmbient (Vector4: rgb, w = 1 when set, 2 for a placed object that moved, whose tint stays its row), HaloObjectLight0, HaloObjectToLight0,
//     HaloObjectLight1, HaloObjectToLight1, HaloObjectReflection (Vector3): the object's Halo
//     lighting (ambient, main light color and the unit direction toward it, bounce light color
//     and the direction toward it, reflection tint), in Halo's gamma space units, for an object
//     without a row. MovingObjectLights sets these on every Halo model's renderer that has no row;
//     a game doesn't need to.
//   HaloCamo (float, 0-1): active camouflage. On the camouflaged variant of a model (the _camo
//     models, drawn with halo1_model_camo.shader: this file compiled with HALO1_MODEL_CAMOUFLAGED,
//     generated by tools/compile-shaders.ps1) the model is drawn after the frame buffer copy and the bent level
//     behind it is blended over its color by this, as Halo blended its camouflage pass over the
//     model (pure refraction at 1). On the normal model, 1 hides it, for the older setup where a
//     second renderer draws the active camouflage material (halo1_active_camouflage.shader) over it.
//   HaloFunctionA-D (float, 0-1): the object's functions (A-D out). A texture animation that
//     follows one (the Scorpion's treads) plays scaled by it, as in Halo; without it, it stays
//     still. The same attributes drive the meter and transparent shaders on the same renderer.
//   HaloModelReflectionTint (float4, scene attribute): Halo's rasterizer_model_ambient_reflection_tint
//     during cinematics. While any of it is above 0, every model's reflection tint is its rgb times
//     its w, the same head on and at glancing angles, in place of the shader's parallel and
//     perpendicular tint and brightness.
//   HaloPointLightReflection (float, 0 or 1): beyond Halo, point lights brighten the object's
//     cube map reflection as well as its diffuse color, so a visor shines in a headlight's beam
//     at night. Off by default; also read from the scene's render attributes, to turn it on for
//     every model at once.
//
// halo1_model_skin.shader is this file compiled with HALO1_MODEL_SKIN (see
// tools/compile-shaders.ps1), for MCC's weapon skins on first person weapons (WeaponSkins). The
// base and multipurpose maps are the skin's, and it adds the physically based maps MCC's menus draw
// the skins with: a tangent space normal map (two channels, Unreal's: x along the tangent, y along
// the binormal), metallic, roughness and emissive. Halo's object lighting still lights it: its
// ambient, main and bounce lights and point lights light the diffuse color (the base map less its
// metal), and the main and bounce lights add broad GGX highlights. What the metal reflects is the
// scene itself where an s&box environment probe covers the model (a game puts a realtime one on its
// view model, so the reflections are of the walls and sky around the player), else Halo's light
// around the object seen in the reflected direction, shaped by the shader's cube map blurred for the
// roughness (SkinReflection); either through the split sum's environment BRDF. The detail map is
// left out: the skins' maps hold their own detail at four times Halo's size.
//

HEADER
{
	Description = "Halo CE model shader";
}

FEATURES
{
	#include "common/features.hlsl"

	Feature( F_TWO_SIDED, 0..1, "Rendering" );
}

MODES
{
	Forward();
	Depth();
	ToolsShadingComplexity( "tools_shading_complexity.shader" );
}

COMMON
{
	// halo1_model_camo.shader defines this: the camouflaged variant is drawn with the translucent
	// things, after the frame buffer copy (see the camouflage inputs in PS).
	#ifndef HALO1_MODEL_CAMOUFLAGED
	#define HALO1_MODEL_CAMOUFLAGED 0
	#endif

	// halo1_model_skin.shader defines this: MCC's weapon skins (see the top of the file).
	#ifndef HALO1_MODEL_SKIN
	#define HALO1_MODEL_SKIN 0
	#endif
	#if HALO1_MODEL_CAMOUFLAGED
	#define S_TRANSLUCENT 1
	#define BLEND_MODE_ALREADY_SET 1
	#define DEPTH_STATE_ALREADY_SET 1
	#endif

	#ifndef S_ALPHA_TEST
	#define S_ALPHA_TEST 1
	#endif
	#ifndef S_TRANSLUCENT
	#define S_TRANSLUCENT 0
	#endif

	#include "common/shared.hlsl"

	#define CUSTOM_MATERIAL_INPUTS
}

struct VertexInput
{
	#include "common/vertexinput.hlsl"
};

struct PixelInput
{
	#include "common/pixelinput.hlsl"

	// Where the object's light is read from the light volume (world, s&box units).
	float3 vHaloLightProbeWs : TEXCOORD14;

	// Which side of a two-sided shader's faces is drawn (see the normal in MainPs).
	#if ( PROGRAM == VFX_PROGRAM_PS )
		bool bFrontFacing : SV_IsFrontFace;
	#endif
};

VS
{
	#include "common/vertex.hlsl"

	float4 g_vHaloLightProbe < Attribute( "HaloLightProbe" ); Default4( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float g_flHaloLightProbeHeight < Default( 25.8 ); >;
	float g_flHaloCamoVs < Attribute( "HaloCamo" ); Default( 0.0 ); >;

	PixelInput MainVs( VertexInput v )
	{
		PixelInput i = ProcessVertex( v );

		// A random number per object, from where it stands, to start its animations at different
		// points the way Halo did, and the object's distance from the camera. Passed in the unused
		// second texture coordinate.
		float3x4 objectToWorld = GetTransformMatrix( v.nInstanceTransformID );
		float3 origin = float3( objectToWorld[0][3], objectToWorld[1][3], objectToWorld[2][3] );
		i.vTextureCoords.zw = float2( frac( sin( dot( floor( origin ), float3( 12.9898, 78.233, 37.719 ) ) ) * 43758.5453 ), distance( origin, g_vCameraPositionWs ) );

		i.vHaloLightProbeWs = g_vHaloLightProbe.w > 0.5 ? g_vHaloLightProbe.xyz : origin + float3( 0, 0, g_flHaloLightProbeHeight );

		i = FinalizeVertex( i );

		// Fully camouflaged: not drawn at all, depth included. Halo kept the depth (it drew the level
		// first, so the colour behind stayed), but s&box lays down every opaque depth before any
		// colour, so the level behind would never be drawn there; the camouflage material covers the
		// unit instead. Every vertex on one point leaves no triangle to draw in any pass (a clip in
		// the pixel shader doesn't reach the depth pass, which skips it for opaque materials).
		#if !HALO1_MODEL_CAMOUFLAGED
			if ( g_flHaloCamoVs >= 1 )
				i.vPositionPs = float4( 0, 0, 0, 1 );
		#endif

		return i;
	}
}

PS
{
	#include "common/pixel.hlsl"

	StaticCombo( S_TWO_SIDED, F_TWO_SIDED, Sys( ALL ) );
	RenderState( CullMode, S_TWO_SIDED ? NONE : DEFAULT );

	#if HALO1_MODEL_CAMOUFLAGED
		// Halo copied the frame before drawing camouflaged units, then blended their camouflage
		// pass, a lookup into that copy bent by a cube map of perturbation vectors, over the model
		// by the camouflage. s&box copies the frame buffer after opaque things draw, so the
		// camouflaged variant is translucent (see COMMON), writing its color as is: drawn after
		// the copy, it finds the level behind the unit there, not the unit.
		BoolAttribute( bWantsFBCopyTexture, true );

		// Translucent shaders write no depth by default, which let the model's own farther parts
		// draw over its nearer ones as dark cracks.
		RenderState( DepthEnable, true );
		RenderState( DepthFunc, GREATER_EQUAL );
		RenderState( DepthWriteEnable, true );

		Texture2D g_tFrameBufferCopyTexture < Attribute( "FrameBufferCopyTexture" ); SrgbRead( false ); >;

		// Halo's camouflage settings from the globals (see ActiveCamouflageMaterial.SetGlobals):
		// the distortion cube map as a 2D atlas of its six faces, refraction amount (320x240
		// pixels), distance falloff (s&box units) and tint, normal and hyper stealth.
		CreateInputTexture2D( Distortion, Linear, 8, "None", "_color", ",0/,0/0", Default4( 0.50, 0.50, 1.00, 0.00 ) );
		Texture2D g_tDistortion < Channel( RGBA, Box( Distortion ), Linear ); OutputFormat( RGBA8888 ); SrgbRead( false ); >;
		float2 g_vCamoRefraction < Default2( 11.0, 9.0 ); >;
		float2 g_vCamoDistanceFalloff < Default2( 2270.0, 929.0 ); >;
		float3 g_vCamoTint < Default3( 0.84, 0.84, 0.91 ); >;
		float3 g_vCamoHyperStealthTint < Default3( 1.0, 1.0, 1.0 ); >;

		float g_flHaloCamo < Attribute( "HaloCamo" ); Default( 0.0 ); >;
		float g_flHaloCamoHyperStealth < Attribute( "HaloCamoHyperStealth" ); Default( 0.0 ); >;

		// Debugging aid, set on the renderer: 1 draws only the shaded model, 2 only the bent level.
		float g_flHaloCamoDebug < Attribute( "HaloCamoDebug" ); Default( 0.0 ); >;
	#endif

	SamplerState g_sWrap < Filter( Anisotropic ); AddressU( WRAP ); AddressV( WRAP ); >;
	SamplerState g_sClamp < Filter( Bilinear ); AddressU( CLAMP ); AddressV( CLAMP ); >;

	CreateInputTexture2D( BaseMap, Linear, 8, "None", "_color", ",0/,0/0", Default4( 1.00, 1.00, 1.00, 1.00 ) );
	CreateInputTexture2D( DetailMap, Linear, 8, "None", "_color", ",0/,0/0", Default4( 0.50, 0.50, 0.50, 1.00 ) );
	CreateInputTexture2D( MultipurposeMap, Linear, 8, "None", "_color", ",0/,0/0", Default4( 1.00, 1.00, 1.00, 1.00 ) );
	CreateInputTexture2D( ReflectionMap, Linear, 8, "None", "_color", ",0/,0/0", Default4( 0.00, 0.00, 0.00, 1.00 ) );
	Texture2D g_tBaseMap < Channel( RGBA, Box( BaseMap ), Linear ); OutputFormat( RGBA8888 ); SrgbRead( false ); >;
	Texture2D g_tDetailMap < Channel( RGBA, Box( DetailMap ), Linear ); OutputFormat( RGBA8888 ); SrgbRead( false ); >;
	Texture2D g_tMultipurposeMap < Channel( RGBA, Box( MultipurposeMap ), Linear ); OutputFormat( RGBA8888 ); SrgbRead( false ); >;
	Texture2D g_tReflectionMap < Channel( RGBA, Box( ReflectionMap ), Linear ); OutputFormat( RGBA8888 ); SrgbRead( false ); >;
	CreateInputTexture2D( ObjectTable, Linear, 8, "None", "_color", ",0/,0/0", Default4( 0.00, 0.00, 0.00, 0.00 ) );
	Texture2D g_tObjectTable < Channel( RGBA, Box( ObjectTable ), Linear ); OutputFormat( RGBA8888 ); SrgbRead( false ); >;

	#if HALO1_MODEL_SKIN
		CreateInputTexture2D( SkinNormal, Linear, 8, "None", "_normal", ",0/,0/0", Default4( 0.50, 0.50, 1.00, 1.00 ) );
		CreateInputTexture2D( SkinMetallic, Linear, 8, "None", "_color", ",0/,0/0", Default4( 0.00, 0.00, 0.00, 1.00 ) );
		CreateInputTexture2D( SkinRoughness, Linear, 8, "None", "_color", ",0/,0/0", Default4( 0.50, 0.50, 0.50, 1.00 ) );
		CreateInputTexture2D( SkinEmissive, Linear, 8, "None", "_color", ",0/,0/0", Default4( 0.00, 0.00, 0.00, 1.00 ) );
		CreateInputTexture2D( SkinReflection, Linear, 8, "None", "_color", ",0/,0/0", Default4( 0.00, 0.00, 0.00, 1.00 ) );
		Texture2D g_tSkinNormal < Channel( RGBA, Box( SkinNormal ), Linear ); OutputFormat( RGBA8888 ); SrgbRead( false ); >;
		Texture2D g_tSkinMetallic < Channel( RGBA, Box( SkinMetallic ), Linear ); OutputFormat( RGBA8888 ); SrgbRead( false ); >;
		Texture2D g_tSkinRoughness < Channel( RGBA, Box( SkinRoughness ), Linear ); OutputFormat( RGBA8888 ); SrgbRead( false ); >;
		Texture2D g_tSkinEmissive < Channel( RGBA, Box( SkinEmissive ), Linear ); OutputFormat( RGBA8888 ); SrgbRead( false ); >;

		// The cube map blurred for roughness 1/n to 1, n rows of six faces (SkinReflection).
		Texture2D g_tSkinReflection < Channel( RGBA, Box( SkinReflection ), Linear ); OutputFormat( RGBA8888 ); SrgbRead( false ); >;
		float g_flSkinReflectionLevels < Default( 5.0 ); >;

		// The cube map's average color (display values), what it's divided by to shape Halo's light
		// in reflections, and how much it shapes them (0 not at all, 1 fully).
		float3 g_vSkinCubeMean < Default3( 0.5, 0.5, 0.5 ); >;
		float g_flSkinCubeDetail < Default( 0.5 ); >;

		// Reflection and highlight brightness, how broad Halo's main and bounce lights are (as a
		// roughness their highlights never go below), and the emissive map's brightness.
		float g_flSkinReflectionScale < Default( 1.0 ); >;
		float g_flSkinHighlight < Default( 1.0 ); >;
		float g_flSkinLightSize < Default( 0.4 ); >;

		float g_flSkinEmissiveScale < Default( 1.0 ); >;

		// The diffuse color's brightness against Halo's (linear light; WeaponSkins.AlbedoScale): the
		// skins' physically based colors are lighter than Halo's textures, which had shading painted in.
		float g_flSkinAlbedoScale < Default( 1.0 ); >;

		// Debugging aid (see the end of MainPs), set on the renderer.
		float g_flSkinDebug < Attribute( "HaloSkinDebug" ); Default( 0.0 ); >;

		// 1 flips the normal map's y, for checking its convention.
		float g_flSkinNormalFlipY < Default( 0.0 ); >;
	#endif

	// Rows in g_tObjectTable (0 when no map has been built), and the light and reflection tint of
	// objects whose row isn't found: the last built map's average.
	float g_flObjectCount < Default( 0.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vFallbackAmbient < Default3( 0.5, 0.5, 0.5 ); >;
	float3 g_vFallbackLight0 < Default3( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vFallbackToLight0 < Default3( 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vFallbackLight1 < Default3( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vFallbackToLight1 < Default3( 0.0, 0.0, -1.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vDefaultReflectionTint < Default3( 0.5, 0.5, 0.5 ); >;

	// The last built map's light volume (ObjectLightVolume): four atlases of the grid's slices,
	// premultiplied by whether a cell is lit (image 0's alpha). Size is (cells across, cells deep,
	// slices, slices per atlas row); min and cell in s&box units.
	float g_flHaloLightVolume < Default( 0.0 ); >;
	CreateInputTexture2D( HaloLightVolume0, Linear, 8, "None", "_color", ",0/,0/0", Default4( 0.00, 0.00, 0.00, 0.00 ) );
	CreateInputTexture2D( HaloLightVolume1, Linear, 8, "None", "_color", ",0/,0/0", Default4( 0.00, 0.00, 0.00, 0.00 ) );
	CreateInputTexture2D( HaloLightVolume2, Linear, 8, "None", "_color", ",0/,0/0", Default4( 0.00, 0.00, 0.00, 0.00 ) );
	CreateInputTexture2D( HaloLightVolume3, Linear, 8, "None", "_color", ",0/,0/0", Default4( 0.00, 0.00, 0.00, 0.00 ) );
	Texture2D g_tHaloLightVolume0 < Channel( RGBA, Box( HaloLightVolume0 ), Linear ); OutputFormat( RGBA8888 ); SrgbRead( false ); >;
	Texture2D g_tHaloLightVolume1 < Channel( RGBA, Box( HaloLightVolume1 ), Linear ); OutputFormat( RGBA8888 ); SrgbRead( false ); >;
	Texture2D g_tHaloLightVolume2 < Channel( RGBA, Box( HaloLightVolume2 ), Linear ); OutputFormat( RGBA8888 ); SrgbRead( false ); >;
	Texture2D g_tHaloLightVolume3 < Channel( RGBA, Box( HaloLightVolume3 ), Linear ); OutputFormat( RGBA8888 ); SrgbRead( false ); >;
	float3 g_vHaloLightVolumeMin < Default3( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vHaloLightVolumeCell < Default3( 1.0, 1.0, 1.0 ); >;
	float4 g_vHaloLightVolumeSize < Default4( 1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0 ); >;

	// Per-object values a game sets (see the top of the file). Change colors below 0 aren't set.
	float3 g_vHaloChangeColorA < Attribute( "HaloChangeColorA" ); Default3( -1.0, -1.0, -1.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vHaloChangeColorB < Attribute( "HaloChangeColorB" ); Default3( -1.0, -1.0, -1.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vHaloChangeColorC < Attribute( "HaloChangeColorC" ); Default3( -1.0, -1.0, -1.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vHaloChangeColorD < Attribute( "HaloChangeColorD" ); Default3( -1.0, -1.0, -1.0 ); >;
	float g_flHaloFunctionA < Attribute( "HaloFunctionA" ); Default( -1.0 ); >;
	float g_flHaloFunctionB < Attribute( "HaloFunctionB" ); Default( -1.0 ); >;
	float g_flHaloFunctionC < Attribute( "HaloFunctionC" ); Default( -1.0 ); >;
	float g_flHaloFunctionD < Attribute( "HaloFunctionD" ); Default( -1.0 ); >;
	float4 g_vHaloObjectAmbient < Attribute( "HaloObjectAmbient" ); Default4( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vHaloObjectLight0 < Attribute( "HaloObjectLight0" ); Default3( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vHaloObjectToLight0 < Attribute( "HaloObjectToLight0" ); Default3( 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vHaloObjectLight1 < Attribute( "HaloObjectLight1" ); Default3( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vHaloObjectToLight1 < Attribute( "HaloObjectToLight1" ); Default3( 0.0, 0.0, -1.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vHaloObjectReflection < Attribute( "HaloObjectReflection" ); Default3( 0.5, 0.5, 0.5 ); >;
	float g_flHaloPointLightReflection < Attribute( "HaloPointLightReflection" ); Default( 0.0 ); >;
	float4 g_vHaloModelReflectionTint < Attribute( "HaloModelReflectionTint" ); Default4( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;

	// Set to 1 from a script to see which objects found their row: green found, blue lit by their
	// renderer's lighting (HaloObjectAmbient), red neither (light volume or average).
	// 2 compares the tint the shader receives with g_vDebugTintBytes (0-255 per channel): red if
	// it arrives as is, green if as sRGB converted to linear, blue if as a 2.2 gamma conversion.
	float g_flDebugObjectLookup < Default( 0.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vDebugTintBytes < Default3( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;

	float2 g_vMapScale < Default2( 1.0, 1.0 ); >;
	float2 g_vDetailScale < Default2( 1.0, 1.0 ); >;
	float g_flDetailFunction < Default( 0.0 ); >;
	float g_flAlphaTested < Default( 1.0 ); >;

	// Which multipurpose map channel holds each mask, as a one-hot RGBA selector.
	float4 g_vReflectionMaskChannel < Default4( 0.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float4 g_vSelfIlluminationMaskChannel < Default4( 0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float4 g_vChangeColorMaskChannel < Default4( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 ); >;
	float4 g_vAuxiliaryMaskChannel < Default4( 1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;

	// Detail mask: x = mask (0 none, 1 reflection, 2 self-illumination, 3 change color, 4 auxiliary),
	// y = 1 to invert it, z = 1 to apply the detail map over the reflection too.
	float3 g_vDetailMask < Default3( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;

	// Change colors: x = which one tints the base map (0 none, 1-4 A-D). A is the renderer's tint.
	float g_flChangeColorSource < Default( 0.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vChangeColorB < Default3( 1.0, 1.0, 1.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vChangeColorC < Default3( 1.0, 1.0, 1.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vChangeColorD < Default3( 1.0, 1.0, 1.0 ); >;

	// Self-illumination: the color animates between the bounds, times a change color (x: 0-4).
	// Animation is (function, period in seconds, random phase 0/1, unused).
	float3 g_vSelfIlluminationLower < Default3( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vSelfIlluminationUpper < Default3( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float4 g_vSelfIlluminationAnimation < Default4( 0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float g_flSelfIlluminationSource < Default( 0.0 ); >;

	// Texture animation, applied to every map: (function, period, phase, scale) each, function
	// -1 for none. The rotation's scale is in degrees, about g_vRotationCenter. The sources are the
	// object function (0 none, 1-4 A-D out) each of U, V and rotation is scaled by.
	float4 g_vUAnimation < Default4( -1.0, 1.0, 0.0, 1.0 ); >;
	float4 g_vVAnimation < Default4( -1.0, 1.0, 0.0, 1.0 ); >;
	float4 g_vRotationAnimation < Default4( -1.0, 1.0, 0.0, 360.0 ); >;
	float2 g_vRotationCenter < Default2( 0.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vAnimationSources < Default3( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;

	// Reflection tint head on and at glancing angles, their brightness (x head on, y glancing), and
	// the distance (s&box units) it starts fading out at and is gone by (x, y; y 0 for never).
	float3 g_vPerpendicularReflection < Default3( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float3 g_vParallelReflection < Default3( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float2 g_vReflectionBrightness < Default2( 0.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float2 g_vReflectionFade < Default2( 0.0, 0.0 ); >;
	float g_flHasReflection < Default( 0.0 ); >;

	// How much of the main light reaches faces turned away from it (the shader's translucency).
	float g_flTranslucency < Default( 0.0 ); >;

	// Two-sided shaders flagged "disable two-sided culling": the distance (s&box units) within which
	// back faces are flipped (see MainPs), or 0 for always.
	float g_flTwoSidedNearDistance < Default( 0.0 ); >;

	float HaloHash( float x )
	{
		return frac( sin( x * 12.9898 ) * 43758.5453 );
	}

	// Smoothly interpolated random values, one per whole t.
	float HaloNoise( float t )
	{
		float i = floor( t );
		float f = frac( t );
		return lerp( HaloHash( i ), HaloHash( i + 1 ), f * f * ( 3 - 2 * f ) );
	}

	// Halo's periodic functions, 0 to 1, of t in periods.
	float HaloFunction( float function, float t )
	{
		if ( function < 0.5 ) return 1;									// one
		if ( function < 1.5 ) return 0;									// zero
		if ( function < 3.5 ) return 0.5 + 0.5 * cos( t * 6.2831853 );		// cosine
		if ( function < 5.5 ) return 1 - abs( frac( t ) * 2 - 1 );			// diagonal wave
		if ( function < 7.5 ) return frac( t );							// slide
		if ( function < 8.5 ) return HaloNoise( t * 4 );					// noise
		if ( function < 9.5 ) return HaloHash( floor( t * 30 ) );				// jitter
		if ( function < 10.5 ) return HaloNoise( t );						// wander
		return pow( saturate( 1 - frac( t ) * 8 ), 2 );					// spark: a quick flash each period
	}

	float HaloAnimate( float4 animation, float phase )
	{
		if ( animation.x < 0 ) return 0;
		return HaloFunction( animation.x, g_flTime / animation.y + animation.z + phase ) * animation.w;
	}

	// How much of an animation plays: all of it, or as much as the object function it follows,
	// which a game sets (nothing until it does).
	float AnimationAmount( float source )
	{
		if ( source < 0.5 ) return 1;
		float value = source < 1.5 ? g_flHaloFunctionA : source < 2.5 ? g_flHaloFunctionB : source < 3.5 ? g_flHaloFunctionC : g_flHaloFunctionD;
		return saturate( value );
	}

	float3 ChangeColor( float source, float3 a, float3 b, float3 c, float3 d )
	{
		if ( source < 0.5 ) return float3( 1, 1, 1 );
		if ( source < 1.5 ) return a;
		if ( source < 2.5 ) return b;
		if ( source < 3.5 ) return c;
		return d;
	}

	// One texel of an object's row in the table: 0 ambient, 1 main light color, 2 direction toward
	// it, 3 reflection tint, 4 bounce light color, 5 direction toward it, 6-9 change colors A-D
	// (texel 9's alpha is the row's own index).
	float4 ObjectTexel( int index, int texel )
	{
		// ObjectLightingTable.ObjectsPerRow and TexelsPerObject.
		return g_tObjectTable.Load( int3( ( index % 128 ) * 10 + texel, index / 128, 0 ) );
	}

	bool IsObjectIndex( int index )
	{
		return index >= 0 && index < (int)g_flObjectCount && abs( ObjectTexel( index, 9 ).w - index ) < 0.5;
	}

	int ObjectIndexFromBytes( float3 channels )
	{
		float3 bytes = round( saturate( channels ) * 255.0 );
		return (int)( bytes.r + bytes.g * 256.0 + bytes.b * 65536.0 + 0.5 );
	}

	float3 LinearToSrgb( float3 c )
	{
		return lerp( 1.055 * pow( max( c, 0.0 ), 1.0 / 2.4 ) - 0.055, c * 12.92, step( c, 0.0031308 ) );
	}

	// The renderer tint holds the object's index, 8 bits per channel, and arrives as is (checked
	// with debug mode 2). Each row stores its own index to check against.
	bool FindObject( float3 tint, out int index )
	{
		index = ObjectIndexFromBytes( tint );
		return IsObjectIndex( index );
	}

	// One slice of the light volume, bilinear between the cells around cell coordinates xy.
	float4 LightVolumeSlice( Texture2D atlas, float2 xy, float slice )
	{
		float2 size = g_vHaloLightVolumeSize.xy;
		float tilesX = g_vHaloLightVolumeSize.w;
		float2 tile = float2( fmod( slice, tilesX ), floor( slice / tilesX ) );
		float2 atlasSize = size * float2( tilesX, ceil( g_vHaloLightVolumeSize.z / tilesX ) );
		float2 texel = tile * size + clamp( xy, 0, size - 1 ) + 0.5;
		return atlas.SampleLevel( g_sClamp, texel / atlasSize, 0 );
	}

	float4 LightVolume( Texture2D atlas, float3 cell, float z0, float z1, float fz )
	{
		return lerp( LightVolumeSlice( atlas, cell.xy, z0 ), LightVolumeSlice( atlas, cell.xy, z1 ), fz );
	}

	// Halo's lighting for a moving object centered at a point, from the light volume: trilinear
	// between the lit cells around it. False where none of them is lit.
	bool SampleLightVolume( float3 positionWs, out float3 ambient, out float3 light0, out float3 toLight0, out float3 light1, out float3 toLight1, out float3 reflectionTint )
	{
		float3 cell = ( positionWs - g_vHaloLightVolumeMin ) / g_vHaloLightVolumeCell - 0.5;
		float z = clamp( cell.z, 0, g_vHaloLightVolumeSize.z - 1 );
		float z0 = floor( z );
		float z1 = min( z0 + 1, g_vHaloLightVolumeSize.z - 1 );
		float fz = z - z0;

		float4 main = LightVolume( g_tHaloLightVolume0, cell, z0, z1, fz );
		float4 direction = LightVolume( g_tHaloLightVolume1, cell, z0, z1, fz );
		float4 bounce = LightVolume( g_tHaloLightVolume2, cell, z0, z1, fz );
		float4 reflection = LightVolume( g_tHaloLightVolume3, cell, z0, z1, fz );

		// Everything is premultiplied by how lit the cells are, so dividing by it leaves the
		// average of the lit ones.
		float lit = main.a;
		float scale = 1 / max( lit, 1.0 / 255.0 );
		light0 = main.rgb * scale;
		ambient = light0 * 0.4 + 0.03;
		toLight0 = normalize( direction.rgb * scale * 2 - 1 + float3( 0, 0, 1e-4 ) );
		light1 = bounce.rgb * scale;
		float2 normalXy = float2( bounce.a, reflection.a ) * scale * 2 - 1;
		toLight1 = -float3( normalXy, sqrt( saturate( 1 - dot( normalXy, normalXy ) ) ) );
		reflectionTint = reflection.rgb * scale;
		return lit > 0.02;
	}

	float3 ApplyDetail( float3 color, float3 detail, float function )
	{
		if ( function < 0.5 ) return saturate( color * detail * 2.0 );
		if ( function < 1.5 ) return color * detail;
		return saturate( color + 2.0 * detail - 1.0 );
	}

	// Cube maps arrive as a 2D atlas of six faces side by side (+X, -X, +Y, -Y, +Z, -Z), and the
	// face and position within it are picked with Direct3D's cube map rules. Same as in
	// halo1_glass_water.shader.
	// Which face of the atlas a direction lands on (x) and where in it (y, z, 0-1).
	float4 SampleCubeAtlasFace( float3 dir )
	{
		float3 a = abs( dir );
		float face;
		float2 st;
		float ma;

		if ( a.x >= a.y && a.x >= a.z )
		{
			ma = a.x;
			face = dir.x > 0 ? 0 : 1;
			st = dir.x > 0 ? float2( -dir.z, -dir.y ) : float2( dir.z, -dir.y );
		}
		else if ( a.y >= a.z )
		{
			ma = a.y;
			face = dir.y > 0 ? 2 : 3;
			st = dir.y > 0 ? float2( dir.x, dir.z ) : float2( dir.x, -dir.z );
		}
		else
		{
			ma = a.z;
			face = dir.z > 0 ? 4 : 5;
			st = dir.z > 0 ? float2( dir.x, -dir.y ) : float2( -dir.x, -dir.y );
		}

		// Keep away from the face edges so filtering doesn't pick up the neighbouring face.
		float2 uv = clamp( st / ma * 0.5 + 0.5, 0.01, 0.99 );
		return float4( face, uv, 0 );
	}

	float4 SampleCubeAtlas( Texture2D atlas, float3 dir )
	{
		float4 face = SampleCubeAtlasFace( dir );
		return atlas.SampleLevel( g_sClamp, float2( ( face.x + face.y ) / 6.0, face.z ), 0 );
	}

	float3 SampleCubeAtlas( float3 dir )
	{
		return SampleCubeAtlas( g_tReflectionMap, dir ).rgb;
	}

	// ShadingModelStandard::Shade, except that the reflection and self-illumination are added to
	// the lit diffuse color in gamma space. Halo added them in its (gamma space) frame buffer, and
	// added in linear space they come out several times weaker next to the lit color: the
	// Warthog lost its sheen, and a Covenant turret's red base won over its purple reflection.
	// Laid out like the custom lighting in core's line.shader.
	//
	// An object with Halo lighting (haloLit) is lit by haloLightGamma alone, in Halo's gamma space
	// units, its point lights included (see HaloPointLights). Other objects get the full engine
	// lighting.
	// Halo's colors are display values; the frame buffer is encoded with the sRGB curve, so they go
	// to linear and back with that curve. A plain 2.2 power is close for mid tones but crushes the
	// darks: Halo 0.05 came out at 4/255 instead of 13, and objects in shade (b30's forest trunks)
	// were near black next to ground drawn with the sRGB curve. Written out to work above 1 (HDR).
	float3 HaloToLinear( float3 c )
	{
		c = max( c, 0 );
		return lerp( pow( ( c + 0.055 ) / 1.055, 2.4 ), c / 12.92, step( c, 0.04045 ) );
	}

	float3 LinearToHalo( float3 c )
	{
		c = max( c, 0 );
		return lerp( 1.055 * pow( c, 1.0 / 2.4 ) - 0.055, c * 12.92, step( c, 0.0031308 ) );
	}

	// Halo CE's atmospheric fog amount: linear in view depth from the start distance to the maximum
	// density at the opaque distance (halo1_environment.shader has the details).
	float HaloFogAmount( float3 positionWs )
	{
		if ( !g_bFogEnabled || !g_bGradientFogEnabled )
			return 0;

		float depth = dot( positionWs - g_vCameraPositionWs, g_vCameraDirWs );
		float u = saturate( g_vGradientFogBiasAndScale.x + g_vGradientFogBiasAndScale.z * depth );
		return u * pow( saturate( g_vGradientFogColor_Opacity.w ), 1.0 / 2.2 );
	}

	// Objects shadowing themselves and each other, which Halo didn't do: the main light, where it comes
	// from the sun's side, is blocked by the sun's shadow map (which only objects cast into; the level's
	// shadows are in the light Halo works out from the lightmap already). Renderers can turn it off with
	// the HaloSunShadows attribute (first person models drawn away from the world, say).
	float g_flHaloSunShadows < Attribute( "HaloSunShadows" ); Default( 1.0 ); >;

	// The sun's shadow, as DirectionalLightShadow::GetVisibility gives it, with three changes:
	// - The cascade is the first whose shadow map square holds the point, not the first whose sphere does. s&box leaves
	//   out of each cascade the casters entirely inside a box at the previous one's center (they're in that one), but a
	//   point under such a caster can be outside the previous sphere: its shadow went missing whenever the camera moved
	//   that boundary across it. Under the box, the previous square always holds it. The point must be inside the
	//   cascade's depth range too, as the sphere made sure of: from high up, a near cascade's square falls on ground
	//   further along the sunlight than its far plane, where nothing on that ground is in its shadow map (a tank on
	//   the far side of another lost its shadow whenever the camera looked down on the two).
	// - s&box's filter is turned by a noise fixed to the screen (for temporal antialiasing), which left a hatching on the
	//   edges; here it gets a fixed screen position, so one fixed turn.
	// - Over the outer 8% of each square it blends into the next, coarser cascade (wider made shadows dip partway: two
	//   offset, differently blurred copies averaged), and the last fades out to no shadow,
	//   instead of stepping (Halo's object shadows faded out with distance too).
	static const float SunCascadeBlend = 0.08;

	float3 SunCascadePosition( uint c, float3 positionWs, out float edge )
	{
		float3 shadowPos = mul( g_DirectionalLightWorldToShadowViewMatrices[c], float4( positionWs, 1 ) ).xyz;
		edge = max( abs( shadowPos.x - 0.5 ), abs( shadowPos.y - 0.5 ) ) * 2;
		// Reversed depth: 1 at the near plane (toward the sun), 0 at the far plane. Outside that, outside the cascade.
		if ( shadowPos.z <= 0 || shadowPos.z >= 1 )
			edge = 2;
		return shadowPos;
	}

	float HaloSunVisibility( float3 positionWs, float3 normalWs, float4 positionSs )
	{
		uint count = min( g_DirectionalLightCascadeCount, MAX_CASCADE_COUNT );

		[loop]
		for ( uint c = 0; c < count; c++ )
		{
			float edge;
			SunCascadePosition( c, positionWs, edge );
			if ( edge >= 0.97 )
				continue;

			float visibility = DirectionalLightShadow::SampleCascade( c, positionWs, normalWs, float2( 0, 0 ) );
			float blend = saturate( ( edge - ( 0.97 - SunCascadeBlend ) ) / SunCascadeBlend );
			if ( blend > 0 )
			{
				float next = 1;
				float nextEdge;
				if ( c + 1 < count )
				{
					SunCascadePosition( c + 1, positionWs, nextEdge );
					if ( nextEdge < 0.97 )
						next = DirectionalLightShadow::SampleCascade( c + 1, positionWs, normalWs, float2( 0, 0 ) );
				}
				visibility = lerp( visibility, next, blend );
			}
			return visibility;
		}

		return 1;
	}

	float HaloObjectSunShadow( float3 positionWs, float3 normalWs, float3 toLight0, float4 positionSs )
	{
		if ( g_flHaloSunShadows < 0.5 || !g_DirectionalLightEnabled )
			return 1;

		float sunward = smoothstep( 0.25, 0.75, dot( toLight0, -normalize( g_DirectionalLightDirection.xyz ) ) );
		if ( sunward <= 0 )
			return 1;

		return lerp( 1, HaloSunVisibility( positionWs, normalWs, positionSs ), sunward );
	}

	// One of Halo's point lights on an object, as its model vertex shaders lit a vertex: the
	// light's color times 1 - d^2/r^2, the spot cone's linear falloff from the cutoff angle in to
	// the falloff angle, and N.L. Diffuse only, and no gel.
	float3 HaloPointLight( BinnedLight data, float3 positionWs, float3 normalWs )
	{
		float3 ray = data.GetPosition() - positionWs;
		float distanceSquared = dot( ray, ray );
		float3 toLight = ray * rsqrt( max( distanceSquared, 1e-8 ) );
		float attenuation = saturate( 1 - distanceSquared / ( data.Radius * data.Radius ) );
		float cone = data.IsSpotLight()
			? saturate( ( dot( toLight, -data.GetDirection() ) - data.SpotLightInnerOuterConeCosines.y ) * data.SpotLightInnerOuterConeCosines.z )
			: 1;
		return data.Color * attenuation * cone * saturate( dot( normalWs, toLight ) );
	}

	// Halo's point lights on an object, in its gamma space units: the two brightest at the
	// object's center (each light's brightness times its 1 - d^2/r^2 there, the dimmer of the two
	// giving way to a brighter one), each lighting the pixel as HaloPointLight.
	float3 HaloPointLights( float3 centerWs, float3 positionWs, float3 normalWs, float4 positionSs )
	{
		ClusterRange range = Cluster::Query( ClusterItemType_Light, positionSs );

		uint count = 0;
		uint first = 0;
		uint second = 0;
		float firstIntensity = 0;
		float secondIntensity = 0;

		[loop]
		for ( uint n = 0; n < range.Count; n++ )
		{
			uint index = Cluster::LoadItem( range, n );
			BinnedLight data = DynamicLightConstantByIndex( index );
			if ( data.Type == LightType::LightTypeDirectional || data.Radius <= 0 )
				continue;

			float3 ray = data.GetPosition() - centerWs;
			float intensity = dot( data.Color, float3( 0.299, 0.587, 0.114 ) ) * ( 1 - dot( ray, ray ) / ( data.Radius * data.Radius ) );

			if ( count == 0 )
			{
				first = index;
				firstIntensity = intensity;
				count = 1;
			}
			else if ( count == 1 )
			{
				second = index;
				secondIntensity = intensity;
				count = 2;
			}
			else if ( firstIntensity <= secondIntensity )
			{
				if ( firstIntensity < intensity )
				{
					first = index;
					firstIntensity = intensity;
				}
			}
			else if ( secondIntensity < intensity )
			{
				second = index;
				secondIntensity = intensity;
			}
		}

		float3 sum = 0;
		if ( count > 0 )
			sum += HaloPointLight( DynamicLightConstantByIndex( first ), positionWs, normalWs );
		if ( count > 1 )
			sum += HaloPointLight( DynamicLightConstantByIndex( second ), positionWs, normalWs );

		// The mount scales Halo's light colors up by pi for s&box's lighting.
		return sum / 3.14159265;
	}

	#if HALO1_MODEL_SKIN
	float3 SampleSkinReflectionRow( float3 dir, float row )
	{
		float4 face = SampleCubeAtlasFace( dir );
		float2 uv = float2( ( face.x + face.y ) / 6.0, ( row + face.z ) / g_flSkinReflectionLevels );
		return g_tSkinReflection.SampleLevel( g_sClamp, uv, 0 ).rgb;
	}

	// The cube map seen by a surface of some roughness: the sharp cube map at 0, the blurred rows
	// above it, blended between levels.
	float3 SampleSkinReflection( float3 dir, float roughness )
	{
		float levels = g_flSkinReflectionLevels;
		float level = saturate( roughness ) * levels;
		float lower = floor( level );
		float3 a = lower < 0.5 ? SampleCubeAtlas( dir ) : SampleSkinReflectionRow( dir, lower - 1 );
		float3 b = lower >= levels ? a : SampleSkinReflectionRow( dir, lower );
		return lerp( a, b, level - lower );
	}

	// GGX highlight of one light: D, the height correlated Smith visibility and Schlick's fresnel,
	// times n.l.
	float3 SkinSpecular( float3 n, float3 v, float3 l, float roughness, float3 f0 )
	{
		float nl = saturate( dot( n, l ) );
		if ( nl <= 0 )
			return 0;

		float3 h = normalize( v + l );
		float nv = max( dot( n, v ), 1e-4 );
		float nh = saturate( dot( n, h ) );
		float a = max( roughness * roughness, 0.002 );
		float a2 = a * a;
		float d = a2 / ( 3.14159265 * pow( nh * nh * ( a2 - 1 ) + 1, 2 ) );
		float vis = 0.5 / ( nl * sqrt( nv * nv * ( 1 - a2 ) + a2 ) + nv * sqrt( nl * nl * ( 1 - a2 ) + a2 ) );
		float3 f = f0 + ( 1 - f0 ) * pow( 1 - saturate( dot( v, h ) ), 5 );
		return d * vis * f * nl;
	}

	// The split sum's environment BRDF, fitted (Karis, "Physically Based Shading on Mobile").
	float3 SkinEnvironmentBrdf( float3 f0, float roughness, float nv )
	{
		const float4 c0 = float4( -1, -0.0275, -0.572, 0.022 );
		const float4 c1 = float4( 1, 0.0425, 1.04, -0.04 );
		float4 r = roughness * c0 + c1;
		float a004 = min( r.x * r.x, exp2( -9.28 * nv ) ) * r.x + r.y;
		float2 ab = float2( -1.04, 1.04 ) * a004 + r.zw;
		return f0 * ab.x + ab.y;
	}
	#endif

	float4 ShadeHalo( Material m, float3 colorGamma, float3 addedGamma, float3 haloLightGamma, bool haloLit, float3 specularLinear = 0 )
	{
		LightingTerms_t lightingTerms = InitLightingTerms();
		CombinerInput combinerInput = ShadingModelStandard::MaterialToCombinerInput( m );

		lightingTerms.vDiffuse = float3( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 );
		lightingTerms.vSpecular = float3( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 );
		lightingTerms.vTransmissive = float3( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 );

		// Uses screen space derivatives, so it's taken outside any branch.
		const float3 vReceiverNormalWs = ComputeShadowReceiverNormal( combinerInput.vPositionWs.xyz );

		if ( !haloLit )
		{
			ComputeDirectionalLight( combinerInput, vReceiverNormalWs, lightingTerms.vDiffuse.rgb, lightingTerms.vSpecular.rgb, lightingTerms.vTransmissive.rgb );

			ClusterRange lightRange = Cluster::Query( ClusterItemType_Light, combinerInput.vPositionSs );

			[loop]
			for ( uint li = 0; li < lightRange.Count; li++ )
			{
				const uint lightIndex = Cluster::LoadItem( lightRange, li );
				const BinnedLight lightData = DynamicLightConstantByIndex( lightIndex );
				ComputeDirectLightingForLight( combinerInput, vReceiverNormalWs, lightData, lightingTerms.vDiffuse.rgb, lightingTerms.vSpecular.rgb, lightingTerms.vTransmissive.rgb );
			}

			CalculateIndirectLighting( lightingTerms, combinerInput );
		}

		float3 vDiffuseAO = CalculateDiffuseAmbientOcclusion( combinerInput, lightingTerms );
		lightingTerms.vIndirectDiffuse.rgb *= vDiffuseAO.rgb;
		lightingTerms.vDiffuse.rgb *= lerp( float3( 1.0, 1.0, 1.0 ), vDiffuseAO.rgb, combinerInput.flAmbientOcclusionDirectDiffuse );

		float3 vSpecularAO = CalculateSpecularAmbientOcclusion( combinerInput, lightingTerms );
		lightingTerms.vIndirectSpecular.rgb *= vSpecularAO.rgb;
		lightingTerms.vSpecular.rgb *= lerp( float3( 1.0, 1.0, 1.0 ), vSpecularAO.rgb, combinerInput.flAmbientOcclusionDirectSpecular );

		float3 vDiffuse = ( lightingTerms.vDiffuse.rgb + lightingTerms.vIndirectDiffuse.rgb ) * combinerInput.vDiffuseColor.rgb;
		float3 vSpecular = lightingTerms.vSpecular.rgb + lightingTerms.vIndirectSpecular.rgb;
		if ( haloLit )
		{
			vDiffuse = HaloToLinear( colorGamma * haloLightGamma );
			vSpecular = specularLinear;
		}

		// Lit diffuse to gamma space and back with the same curve, so it's unchanged where nothing is added.
		float3 vDiffuseGamma = LinearToHalo( vDiffuse );
		vDiffuse = HaloToLinear( vDiffuseGamma + addedGamma );

		float4 color = float4( vDiffuse + vSpecular, m.Opacity );

		if ( DepthNormals::WantsDepthNormals() )
			return DepthNormals::Output( m.Normal, m.Roughness, color.a );

		if ( ToolsVis::WantsToolsVis() )
			return ShadingModelStandard::DoToolsVis( color, m, lightingTerms );

		// Halo's atmospheric fog blends in gamma space (as in halo1_environment.shader).
		color.rgb = HaloToLinear( lerp( LinearToHalo( color.rgb ), LinearToHalo( g_vGradientFogColor_Opacity.rgb ), HaloFogAmount( m.WorldPosition ) ) );
		return color;
	}

	#if HALO1_MODEL_CAMOUFLAGED
	// The level behind a camouflaged unit, as Halo's camouflage pass drew it: the frame copy read
	// off the pixel's own position by a perturbation looked up by the surface's normal as the
	// camera sees it, the refraction amount times the camouflage, less with distance, tinted.
	float3 Camouflage( float2 positionSs, float3 positionWs, float3 normal, float camo )
	{
		float3 forward = g_vCameraDirWs.xyz;
		float3 right = normalize( cross( forward, g_vCameraUpDirWs.xyz ) );
		float3 up = cross( right, forward );
		float3 viewNormal = float3( dot( normal, right ), dot( normal, up ), -dot( normal, forward ) );

		float hyper = saturate( g_flHaloCamoHyperStealth );
		float refraction = lerp( g_vCamoRefraction.x, g_vCamoRefraction.y, hyper );
		float falloff = lerp( g_vCamoDistanceFalloff.x, g_vCamoDistanceFalloff.y, hyper );
		float3 tint = lerp( g_vCamoTint, g_vCamoHyperStealthTint, hyper );

		float2 perturbation = SampleCubeAtlas( g_tDistortion, viewNormal ).rg * 2 - 1;
		float depth = max( dot( positionWs - g_vCameraPositionWs, forward ), 1 );
		float2 offset = perturbation * refraction * camo * saturate( falloff / depth ) / float2( 320, -240 );

		float2 screenUv = positionSs * g_vInvViewportSize + offset;
		float3 behind = g_tFrameBufferCopyTexture.SampleLevel( g_sClamp, screenUv * g_vFrameBufferCopyInvSizeAndUvScale.zw, 0 ).rgb;

		// s&box's frame copy holds a thin dark rim along a camouflaged unit's outline (most likely a
		// screen space effect darkening the level around the unit's depth), and near the silhouette
		// the bend changes fast enough to stretch it into dark slivers. A bent read far darker than
		// the unbent one takes the unbent one instead.
		float3 straight = g_tFrameBufferCopyTexture.SampleLevel( g_sClamp, positionSs * g_vInvViewportSize * g_vFrameBufferCopyInvSizeAndUvScale.zw, 0 ).rgb;
		if ( max( behind.r, max( behind.g, behind.b ) ) < 0.1 * max( straight.r, max( straight.g, straight.b ) ) )
			behind = straight;

		// Halo tinted the copy in its gamma space frame buffer.
		tint = max( tint, 0 );
		return behind * lerp( pow( ( tint + 0.055 ) / 1.055, 2.4 ), tint / 12.92, step( tint, 0.04045 ) );
	}
	#endif

	float4 MainPs( PixelInput i ) : SV_Target0
	{
		float phase = i.vTextureCoords.z;

		// Texture animation: rotate about the center, then scroll.
		float2 uv = i.vTextureCoords.xy;
		float angle = radians( HaloAnimate( g_vRotationAnimation, 0 ) * AnimationAmount( g_vAnimationSources.z ) );
		if ( angle != 0 )
		{
			float2 d = uv - g_vRotationCenter;
			uv = g_vRotationCenter + float2( d.x * cos( angle ) - d.y * sin( angle ), d.x * sin( angle ) + d.y * cos( angle ) );
		}
		uv += float2( HaloAnimate( g_vUAnimation, 0 ) * AnimationAmount( g_vAnimationSources.x ), HaloAnimate( g_vVAnimation, 0 ) * AnimationAmount( g_vAnimationSources.y ) );
		uv *= g_vMapScale;

		float4 base = Tex2DS( g_tBaseMap, g_sWrap, uv );
		if ( g_flAlphaTested > 0.5 )
			clip( base.a - 0.5 );


		float4 multipurpose = Tex2DS( g_tMultipurposeMap, g_sWrap, uv );
		float reflectionMask = dot( multipurpose, g_vReflectionMaskChannel );
		float selfIlluminationMask = dot( multipurpose, g_vSelfIlluminationMaskChannel );
		float changeColorMask = dot( multipurpose, g_vChangeColorMaskChannel );
		float auxiliaryMask = dot( multipurpose, g_vAuxiliaryMaskChannel );

		// The object's row in the table, if it has one (see the top of the file).
		// Once a map has been built every object is lit Halo's way, by its own row's light, the
		// lighting on its renderer or the map's; only without a table (no map built) and with no
		// lighting on the renderer does the scene's lighting take over.
		int objectIndex = 0;
		bool objectLight = g_vHaloObjectAmbient.w > 0.5;
		bool haloLit = g_flObjectCount > 0.5 || objectLight;
		// Lighting on the renderer wins over the tint. The row is only looked up when the renderer
		// has no lighting, or w = 2 says its tint is a row (a placed object that moved, lit by
		// MovingObjectLights but keeping its row's change colors): a game's tint that ever decoded
		// to some row (a skinned draw's tint hasn't been checked in game) would swap in another
		// object's change colors.
		bool foundObject = false;
		if ( haloLit && ( !objectLight || g_vHaloObjectAmbient.w > 1.5 ) )
			foundObject = FindObject( i.vVertexColor.rgb, objectIndex );

		if ( g_flDebugObjectLookup > 1.5 )
		{
			float3 raw = round( saturate( i.vVertexColor.rgb ) * 255.0 );
			float3 srgb = round( saturate( LinearToSrgb( i.vVertexColor.rgb ) ) * 255.0 );
			float3 gamma = round( saturate( pow( max( i.vVertexColor.rgb, 0.0 ), 1.0 / 2.2 ) ) * 255.0 );
			return float4( all( raw == g_vDebugTintBytes ) ? 1 : 0, all( srgb == g_vDebugTintBytes ) ? 1 : 0, all( gamma == g_vDebugTintBytes ) ? 1 : 0, 1 );
		}
		if ( g_flDebugObjectLookup > 0.5 )
			return float4( foundObject ? float3( 0, 1, 0 ) : objectLight ? float3( 0, 0, 1 ) : float3( 1, 0, 0 ), 1 );

		float3 changeColorA = float3( 1, 1, 1 );
		float3 changeColorB = g_vChangeColorB;
		float3 changeColorC = g_vChangeColorC;
		float3 changeColorD = g_vChangeColorD;
		float3 ambient = g_vFallbackAmbient;
		float3 light0 = g_vFallbackLight0;
		float3 toLight0 = g_vFallbackToLight0;
		float3 light1 = g_vFallbackLight1;
		float3 toLight1 = g_vFallbackToLight1;
		float3 reflectionTint = g_vDefaultReflectionTint;
		if ( foundObject && !objectLight )
		{
			ambient = ObjectTexel( objectIndex, 0 ).rgb;
			light0 = ObjectTexel( objectIndex, 1 ).rgb;
			toLight0 = ObjectTexel( objectIndex, 2 ).xyz;
			reflectionTint = ObjectTexel( objectIndex, 3 ).rgb;
			light1 = ObjectTexel( objectIndex, 4 ).rgb;
			toLight1 = ObjectTexel( objectIndex, 5 ).xyz;
		}
		if ( foundObject )
		{
			changeColorA = ObjectTexel( objectIndex, 6 ).rgb;
			changeColorB = ObjectTexel( objectIndex, 7 ).rgb;
			changeColorC = ObjectTexel( objectIndex, 8 ).rgb;
			changeColorD = ObjectTexel( objectIndex, 9 ).rgb;
		}
		if ( objectLight )
		{
			ambient = g_vHaloObjectAmbient.rgb;
			light0 = g_vHaloObjectLight0;
			toLight0 = g_vHaloObjectToLight0;
			light1 = g_vHaloObjectLight1;
			toLight1 = g_vHaloObjectToLight1;
			reflectionTint = g_vHaloObjectReflection;
		}
		else if ( !foundObject && haloLit && g_flHaloLightVolume > 0.5 )
		{
			float3 a, l0, t0, l1, t1, r;
			if ( SampleLightVolume( i.vHaloLightProbeWs, a, l0, t0, l1, t1, r ) )
			{
				ambient = a;
				light0 = l0;
				toLight0 = t0;
				light1 = l1;
				toLight1 = t1;
				reflectionTint = r;
			}
		}

		// Without a row, a tint other than white is the object's color: it replaces all four change
		// colors, so it reaches whichever one a shader uses (an elite's armor is A, the Chief's C).
		// The tint may instead be a row index that didn't decode while the table is in use and the
		// renderer has no lighting of its own, so then it's ignored.
		float3 tint = i.vVertexColor.rgb;
		if ( !foundObject && ( !haloLit || objectLight ) && any( abs( tint - 1 ) > 0.002 ) )
		{
			changeColorA = tint;
			changeColorB = tint;
			changeColorC = tint;
			changeColorD = tint;
		}

		// A game's own change colors for this object, over the tint.
		if ( g_vHaloChangeColorA.x >= 0 ) changeColorA = g_vHaloChangeColorA;
		if ( g_vHaloChangeColorB.x >= 0 ) changeColorB = g_vHaloChangeColorB;
		if ( g_vHaloChangeColorC.x >= 0 ) changeColorC = g_vHaloChangeColorC;
		if ( g_vHaloChangeColorD.x >= 0 ) changeColorD = g_vHaloChangeColorD;

		// Change color multiplies the base map where its mask is set.
		float3 color = lerp( base.rgb, base.rgb * ChangeColor( g_flChangeColorSource, changeColorA, changeColorB, changeColorC, changeColorD ), changeColorMask );

		// Detail map, limited to one of the masks.
		float detailMask = 1;
		if ( g_vDetailMask.x > 0.5 )
		{
			detailMask = g_vDetailMask.x < 1.5 ? reflectionMask
				: g_vDetailMask.x < 2.5 ? selfIlluminationMask
				: g_vDetailMask.x < 3.5 ? changeColorMask
				: auxiliaryMask;
			if ( g_vDetailMask.y > 0.5 )
				detailMask = 1 - detailMask;
		}

		#if HALO1_MODEL_SKIN
			float3 detail = 0.5;
		#else
			float3 detail = Tex2DS( g_tDetailMap, g_sWrap, uv * g_vDetailScale ).rgb;
			color = lerp( color, ApplyDetail( color, detail, g_flDetailFunction ), detailMask );
		#endif

		// Self-illumination is light added to the diffuse light, so it's multiplied by the color.
		float illuminationPhase = g_vSelfIlluminationAnimation.z > 0.5 ? phase : 0;
		float illuminationT = HaloFunction( g_vSelfIlluminationAnimation.x, g_flTime / g_vSelfIlluminationAnimation.y + illuminationPhase );
		float3 selfIllumination = lerp( g_vSelfIlluminationLower, g_vSelfIlluminationUpper, illuminationT )
			* ChangeColor( g_flSelfIlluminationSource, changeColorA, changeColorB, changeColorC, changeColorD );

		// Added to the lit color in gamma space, below.
		float3 added = saturate( color * selfIllumination * selfIlluminationMask );

		#if HALO1_MODEL_SKIN
			// A skin's emissive map (its lights and glowing paint), where it's brighter than Halo's
			// self-illumination, so the two don't add up on the parts both light.
			added = max( added, saturate( Tex2DS( g_tSkinEmissive, g_sWrap, uv ).rgb * g_flSkinEmissiveScale ) );
		#endif

		float3 positionWs = i.vPositionWithOffsetWs.xyz + g_vHighPrecisionLightingOffsetWs.xyz;
		float3 normal = normalize( i.vNormalWs );

		// Halo draws a two-sided shader twice, the second time culling the other way with the
		// normal flipped, so the back of a face is lit, tinted and reflects as if it faced out.
		// Shaders flagged "disable two-sided culling" only get that second pass while the object
		// is within 8 world units of the camera; further away they're drawn once without culling,
		// so the back of a face keeps the front's normal.
		#if S_TWO_SIDED
			if ( !i.bFrontFacing && ( g_flTwoSidedNearDistance <= 0 || i.vTextureCoords.w <= g_flTwoSidedNearDistance ) )
				normal = -normal;
		#endif

		#if HALO1_MODEL_SKIN
			// Two channels, z rebuilt. The model's binormal is the direction V grows in, which is
			// where Unreal's normal maps point y.
			float2 bentXy = Tex2DS( g_tSkinNormal, g_sWrap, uv ).rg * 2 - 1;
			if ( g_flSkinNormalFlipY > 0.5 )
				bentXy.y = -bentXy.y;
			float3 bent = float3( bentXy, sqrt( saturate( 1 - dot( bentXy, bentXy ) ) ) );
			normal = normalize( bent.x * normalize( i.vTangentUWs ) + bent.y * normalize( i.vTangentVWs ) + bent.z * normal );
		#endif

		float3 pointLight = 0;
		if ( haloLit )
			pointLight = HaloPointLights( i.vHaloLightProbeWs, positionWs, normal, i.vPositionSs );

		// Beyond Halo, when a game asks for it (HaloPointLightReflection): Halo's reflection tint
		// only follows the lightmap, so a visor stays dull in a headlight's beam at night. This lets
		// the point lights reaching the surface brighten it as well, sat( 2 P ) sat( 1.5 lum( P ) ),
		// never below what the lightmap gives.
		if ( g_flHaloPointLightReflection > 0.5 )
			reflectionTint = max( reflectionTint, saturate( pointLight * 2 ) * saturate( dot( pointLight, float3( 0.299, 0.587, 0.114 ) ) * 1.5 ) );

		// Reflection: the cube map seen in the surface, tinted and brightened differently head on
		// and at glancing angles, and faded out with distance. It's scaled by the reflection tint of
		// the ground the object stands on (see ObjectLight.DefaultReflectionTint). Like Halo's model
		// vertex shaders, the tint and the brightness each go linearly with N.V (V toward the camera),
		// saturated, from parallel at N.V = 0 to perpendicular head on.
		if ( !HALO1_MODEL_SKIN && g_flHasReflection > 0.5 )
		{
			float3 toCamera = g_vCameraPositionWs - positionWs;
			float dist = length( toCamera );
			toCamera /= max( dist, 0.001 );

			float facing = saturate( dot( normal, toCamera ) );
			float3 tint = any( g_vHaloModelReflectionTint > 0 )
				? g_vHaloModelReflectionTint.rgb * g_vHaloModelReflectionTint.a
				: saturate( lerp( g_vParallelReflection, g_vPerpendicularReflection, facing ) )
					* saturate( lerp( g_vReflectionBrightness.y, g_vReflectionBrightness.x, facing ) );
			float fade = g_vReflectionFade.y > g_vReflectionFade.x ? 1 - saturate( ( dist - g_vReflectionFade.x ) / ( g_vReflectionFade.y - g_vReflectionFade.x ) ) : 1;

			float3 reflection = SampleCubeAtlas( reflect( -toCamera, normal ) ) * tint * reflectionTint * reflectionMask * fade;

			// Detail after reflection: the detail map darkens or brightens the reflection as well.
			if ( g_vDetailMask.z > 0.5 )
				reflection = lerp( reflection, ApplyDetail( reflection, detail, g_flDetailFunction ), detailMask );

			added += saturate( reflection );
		}

		Material m = Material::From( i );
		m.Albedo = SrgbGammaToLinear( color );
		m.Normal = normal;
		m.Roughness = 0.85;
		m.Metalness = 0;
		m.AmbientOcclusion = 1;
		m.TintMask = 1;
		m.Opacity = 1;
		m.Emission = float3( 0, 0, 0 );
		m.Transmission = 0;
		m.WorldTangentU = i.vTangentUWs;
		m.WorldTangentV = i.vTangentVWs;
		m.TextureCoords = uv;

		// What ShadingModelStandard::Shade( i, m ) fills in from the pixel input.
		m.WorldPositionWithOffset = i.vPositionWithOffsetWs;
		m.WorldPosition = positionWs;
		m.ScreenPosition = i.vPositionSs;
		m.GeometricNormal = i.vNormalWs.xyz;

		#if S_MODE_DEPTH
			return ShadingModelStandard::Shade( m );
		#endif

		// Halo's object lighting, as its model vertex shaders do it (per pixel here): ambient, the
		// main light and the bounce light from below, each on the side facing it, and the point
		// lights, clamped to 1. The shader's translucency lets the main light through to the side
		// turned away from it: saturate( max( n.l, -n.l * translucency ) ).
		light0 *= HaloObjectSunShadow( positionWs, normalize( i.vNormalWs.xyz ), toLight0, i.vPositionSs );
		float facingLight0 = dot( normal, toLight0 );
		facingLight0 = max( facingLight0, -facingLight0 * g_flTranslucency );
		float3 haloLight = saturate( ambient + light0 * saturate( facingLight0 ) + light1 * saturate( dot( normal, toLight1 ) ) + pointLight );

		#if HALO1_MODEL_SKIN
			// Metal has no diffuse color, only highlights and reflections tinted by its own color;
			// everything else reflects 4% head on.
			float metal = saturate( Tex2DS( g_tSkinMetallic, g_sWrap, uv ).r );
			float roughness = clamp( Tex2DS( g_tSkinRoughness, g_sWrap, uv ).r, 0.02, 1.0 );
			float3 f0 = lerp( 0.04, HaloToLinear( color ), metal );
			float3 diffuseColor = LinearToHalo( HaloToLinear( color ) * g_flSkinAlbedoScale ) * ( 1 - metal );
			float3 toEye = normalize( g_vCameraPositionWs - positionWs );
			float facingEye = max( dot( normal, toEye ), 1e-4 );
			float3 reflected = reflect( -toEye, normal );

			// Highlights of Halo's main and bounce lights. They're Halo's guess at where the light
			// around the object comes from (worked out from the lightmap), not suns, so each is a
			// broad light: its lobe widened as if the surface were at least that rough.
			float lightRoughness = sqrt( saturate( roughness * roughness + g_flSkinLightSize * g_flSkinLightSize ) );
			float3 specular = ( SkinSpecular( normal, toEye, toLight0, lightRoughness, f0 ) * HaloToLinear( light0 )
				+ SkinSpecular( normal, toEye, toLight1, lightRoughness, f0 ) * HaloToLinear( light1 ) ) * g_flSkinHighlight;

			// What the surface reflects: the scene around it, where an environment probe covers it
			// (the game puts a realtime one on the view model), so the walls, the sky and their light
			// show in the metal. Otherwise Halo's light around the object, seen in the direction
			// reflected: its ambient, and its main and bounce lights from the sides they're on, with
			// the cube map only shaping it (its light and dark, divided by its average).
			float3 environment;
			ClusterRange probes = Cluster::Query( ClusterItemType_EnvMap, i.vPositionSs );
			if ( probes.Count > 0 )
			{
				environment = EnvMap::From( positionWs, i.vPositionSs, normal, roughness * roughness );
			}
			else
			{
				float3 lightSeen = saturate( ambient + light0 * saturate( dot( reflected, toLight0 ) ) + light1 * saturate( dot( reflected, toLight1 ) ) + pointLight );
				float3 cubeShape = lerp( 1, SampleSkinReflection( reflected, roughness ) / max( g_vSkinCubeMean, 0.05 ), g_flSkinCubeDetail );
				environment = HaloToLinear( saturate( lightSeen * cubeShape ) );
			}
			specular += environment * g_flSkinReflectionScale * SkinEnvironmentBrdf( f0, roughness, facingEye );

			// The scene's own lighting, before any map is built.
			m.Albedo = SrgbGammaToLinear( color );
			m.Metalness = metal;
			m.Roughness = roughness;

			float4 result = ShadeHalo( m, diffuseColor, added, haloLight, haloLit, specular );

			// Debugging aid, set on the renderer: 1 the diffuse part alone, 2 highlights and
			// reflections alone, 3 the environment reflected, 4 metal, 5 roughness.
			int skinDebug = (int)round( g_flSkinDebug );
			if ( skinDebug == 1 ) result.rgb = HaloToLinear( diffuseColor * haloLight );
			else if ( skinDebug == 2 ) result.rgb = specular;
			else if ( skinDebug == 3 ) result.rgb = environment;
			else if ( skinDebug == 4 ) result.rgb = metal;
			else if ( skinDebug == 5 ) result.rgb = roughness;
		#else
			float4 result = ShadeHalo( m, color, added, haloLight, haloLit );
		#endif

		#if HALO1_MODEL_CAMOUFLAGED
			float camo = saturate( g_flHaloCamo );
			int debug = (int)round( g_flHaloCamoDebug );
			if ( debug == 1 )
				camo = 0;
			else if ( debug == 2 )
				camo = 1;
			result = float4( lerp( result.rgb, Camouflage( i.vPositionSs.xy, positionWs, normal, camo ), camo ), 1 );
		#endif

		return result;
	}
}
